{"type": "say", "text": "Elena, let me provide my final recommendation as employment counsel on all three open items, incorporating the new information Maya and Thomas have put on the record. First, on executive-interview protocol — given that the implicated vice president has retained separate counsel and will not participate in a company interview without that counsel's written consent, I recommend we propose a protocol to DOJ that explicitly accounts for this: any interview of the VP will be conducted only with his separate counsel present and only after that counsel has received the engagement letter and provided written consent to participate. We should also make clear in our protocol that document review precedes any interviews and that interview scope will be defined in advance. This is not a limitation we can work around — it's a hard constraint, and our protocol should reflect it transparently. Second, on voluntary disclosure — from an employment counsel perspective, I support Maya's lean toward voluntary disclosure. We have a confirmed $275,000 in improper payments, and once we can verify that figure falls within Thomas's four-distributor, 2019–2021 scope, I think disclosure is the right call. It positions us credibly and allows us to frame the remediation, including any personnel actions, on our terms rather than reactively. Third, on the repayment amount — if Maya's verification confirms the $275,000 falls within the subpoena's scope, I concur with recommending $275,000 as the proposed repayment figure. I'd add that from an employment standpoint, we should also be prepared to address any personnel consequences related to the implicated VP as part of our overall remediation plan, and I'd advise we coordinate any such actions carefully given his separate counsel situation. Those are my final recommendations on all three items, contingent on Maya's scope verification."}
